    <section class="doc-section"><h2>What happens on GPU</h2><p>On every tier, the forward and backward operations run as TypeShade kernel functions, each <code>data()</code> call as one fuse scope. An optimizer reads the gradients of all its parameters in one plan. SGD and Adam keep their moment state and update the parameters on host arrays, so training throughput is not tuned yet.</p><div class="note"><strong>Explicit reads remain reads.</strong><span>Calling <code>loss.data()</code> or <code>parameter.data()</code> requests a host result. The sample loop above does not read the full parameter or gradient arrays back each step.</span></div></section>
    <section class="doc-section"><h2>Dispose each batch graph</h2><p><code>disposeGraph(model.parameters())</code> releases temporary tensors while retaining model parameters. Keep the Device alive for the training session and dispose it after the final batch and any pending work finish.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/typeshade/typenn/tree/main/examples/mnist">Open the full MNIST example ↗</a></p></section>
